WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.710
Plain HTTP is readable text.

2
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.729
Every router between you and the server passes it along.

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:16.449
Whoever runs one of them, or shares an open café Wi-Fi with you, could read your password.

4
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:19.892
Or change the page on its way back.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.077
HTTPS is HTTP inside a TLS connection.

6
00:00:25.077 --> 00:00:28.560
The same request, sealed on its way.

7
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:30.757
TLS gives you three things.

8
00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:32.452
First, confidentiality.

9
00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:35.400
Nobody on the path can read the traffic.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:38.073
Second, integrity.

11
00:00:38.073 --> 00:00:41.860
Nobody can change it without the change being detected.

12
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:43.500
Third, authentication.

13
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.843
The server proves it holds the private key for a certificate that names this host.

14
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:55.530
Before any HTTP flows, the client and the server run a handshake.

15
00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.500
In TLS 1.3, it takes one round trip.

16
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:00.160
The client says hello.

17
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:06.384
It lists the ciphers it supports, sends its half of a key exchange, and names the host it wants.

18
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.097
The server answers with its own hello, and its half of the key exchange.

19
00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:18.155
Then, already encrypted, it sends its certificate, and a signature that proves it holds the matching private key.

20
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:21.839
How can two machines agree on a key while others listen?

21
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:26.551
Each side keeps a random secret, and sends only a public value made from it.

22
00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:30.909
Each side combines its own secret with the other's public value.

23
00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:32.721
Both arrive at the same key.

24
00:01:32.721 --> 00:01:36.216
The public values alone are not enough to work it out.

25
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:38.774
The client checks the certificate.

26
00:01:38.774 --> 00:01:39.807
The right name.

27
00:01:39.807 --> 00:01:40.633
Still valid.

28
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.561
Signed by someone it trusts.

29
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:47.808
From here on, everything, including the HTTP request, is encrypted.

30
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:53.186
But a signature only proves the server holds some private key.

31
00:01:53.186 --> 00:01:55.866
Who says that key belongs to example.com?

32
00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:59.265
A certificate, signed by someone the browser trusts.

33
00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:01.679
A certificate is a signed statement.

34
00:02:01.679 --> 00:02:05.859
This public key belongs to these names, from this date to that date.

35
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:12.708
A certificate authority, a CA, signs it, after checking that the requester controls those names.

36
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.565
Your operating system and browser ship a trust store.

37
00:02:17.565 --> 00:02:19.621
A list of root CAs they trust.

38
00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:22.816
Roots rarely sign server certificates directly.

39
00:02:22.816 --> 00:02:28.209
A root signs an intermediate CA, and the intermediate signs the server's certificate.

40
00:02:28.433 --> 00:02:31.576
That lets the root keys stay offline, locked away.

41
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.138
The server sends its certificate, plus the intermediates.

42
00:02:35.138 --> 00:02:38.768
The client follows that chain up, to a root it already trusts.

43
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.491
If any link is missing, expired, or made out to another name, the connection fails.

44
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.578
In production, a request rarely goes straight to your app.

45
00:02:49.578 --> 00:02:55.051
A reverse proxy, such as nginx, or a load balancer, sits in front of the app copies.

46
00:02:55.267 --> 00:03:00.290
The proxy holds the certificate, and runs the handshake with the client.

47
00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:01.337
TLS ends there.

48
00:03:01.337 --> 00:03:03.500
This is called TLS termination.

49
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:12.133
Behind it, the proxy talks plain HTTP to the app copies on port 8000, and spreads the requests across them.

50
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:16.175
It adds headers, so the app still knows the truth.

51
00:03:16.175 --> 00:03:22.727
X-Forwarded-Proto says the client used HTTPS, even though the app only saw plain HTTP.

52
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:28.700
And if no copy answers, the proxy itself replies: 502, Bad Gateway.

53
00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:34.123
So: TLS encrypts the traffic, and proves control of the name.

54
00:03:34.123 --> 00:03:38.078
The certificate chain leads up to a root you already trust.

55
00:03:38.078 --> 00:03:42.704
And in production, TLS often ends at the proxy in front of your apps.
